<div class="prose max-w-prose">

Something Store is a UK direct-to-consumer shop for stickers and 3D prints —
and the place the "paper sketchbook" look on this site started.

## The look

Hard 2px borders, offset drop shadows, a dotted grid background that reads
like graph paper, and hand-drawn headings (Nerko One over Playpen Sans body
text). Buttons and cards sit "raised" off the page on a solid shadow and
press flat into it on hover or tap — a small, consistent piece of physicality
that ties every interactive element together without relying on a single
line of animation JavaScript.

## The system

Every colour — mauve, gold, teal, plus success/warning/error — is a full
50–950 ramp, vivid end first, dark end last, defined once as Tailwind v4
theme tokens. Depth is three reusable utilities (`.raised`, `.pressed`,
`.smush`) rather than one-off shadows scattered through components, so a
button, a nav item and a product card all move the same way.

## Built on the platform

The storefront itself (SvelteKit 5 + Tailwind v4) leans on modern CSS and
HTML before reaching for JavaScript: the Popover API and CSS anchor
positioning for menus and dropdowns, `<dialog>` for modals, `:has()` and
container queries where they fit. Accessibility comes first — every
interactive piece works from a keyboard and is announced correctly to a
screen reader — and semantic HTML does as much of the work as it can before
a `<div>` shows up.

This site borrows that same system directly: the colour tokens, the depth
utilities, the fonts, the "press it flat" interaction — just pointed at a
portfolio instead of a shopfront.

</div>
